---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Ambiguous = `Here is the sign-up sheet from the open evening. People typed
their own names in and it shows. I need to know how many
actual different people signed up.`;

const Questions = `Questions this brief does not answer

  - Is "chen, ines" the same person as "CHEN, INES"?
  - Is "okafor,ravi" the same as "Okafor, Ravi"?
  - An entry that is just "?" -- drop it, or count it?
  - What about a blank line?
  - Two real people genuinely called Ravi Okafor. Then what?

MY DECISIONS
  - Case does not matter. Spacing does not matter.
  - An entry with no comma is not a name. Dropped.
  - Every drop gets printed with a reason.
  - Two identical real names WILL be merged. This is wrong
    and I am saying so rather than pretending it cannot happen.`;

const pool = [
  { slug: "cc-byte-reader", title: "Byte Reader", stars: 1, after: "2.14" },
  { slug: "cc-storage-estimate", title: "Storage Estimate", stars: 2, after: "2.15" },
  { slug: "cc-overflow-guard", title: "Overflow Guard", stars: 2, after: "2.16" },
  { slug: "cc-compression-picker", title: "Compression Picker", stars: 2, after: "2.17" },
  { slug: "cc-metadata-search", title: "Metadata Search", stars: 2, after: "2.18" },
  { slug: "cc-clean-the-list", title: "Clean the List", stars: 3, after: "2.19" },
];

const bonus = [
  { slug: "cc-sample-rate", title: "Sample Rate", stars: 3, after: "2.16" },
  { slug: "cc-correlation-check", title: "Correlation Check", stars: 3, after: "2.18" },
];
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge Set 7</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">Complete any 5 of the 6 below.</h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Set Is For</h2>

          <p>
            <b>This is the smallest set in the course, and deliberately so.</b>{" "}
            Most of the Data block is reasoning rather than code, and forcing
            twelve code challenges out of <i>what a chart hides</i> would produce
            filler.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set 5 was the shape of a procedure. Set 6 was picking the algorithm.
            <b> This one is about the answer you hand back.</b>
          </p>

          <p>
            Every brief here asks for a number that somebody is going to act on
            &mdash; buy storage, trust a count, put a claim in a presentation. So
            three of them explicitly require <b>a recommendation, not a table</b>,
            and one of them requires you to name a case where your own rule gets
            it wrong.
          </p>

          <p>
            Same rule as always: <b>write your requirements list before you write
            any code</b>, and submit both.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="shape">
          <h2>The Data Is Messier Than the Brief Admits</h2>

          <p>Here is the whole Clean the List brief:</p>

          <Code
            code={Ambiguous}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="From the Clean the List brief"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <i>How many actual different people</i> sounds like a counting problem.
            It is a <b>deciding</b> problem, and the deciding all happens before
            you count:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Questions}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Requirements — the questions half"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>Read the last decision again.</b> The rule merges two real people
            who happen to share a name, and the requirements list says so out loud
            rather than hoping nobody notices.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>That is the habit this set is built to teach.</b> Activity 2.19's
            filter threw away three ages and moved an average by 118 years. Every
            cleaning rule discards something. Naming what yours discards is the
            difference between a result and a claim.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="recommend">
          <h2>Three of These Want a Recommendation</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Compression Picker</b>, <b>Storage Estimate</b>, and{" "}
            <b>Sample Rate</b> all ask you to end with advice.
          </p>

          <p>
            A table of ratios is not an answer to <i>when should I bother</i>. A
            column of byte counts is not an answer to <i>how much should I buy</i>.
            <b> You have to pick a number and defend it</b>, and your requirements
            list is where the defense goes.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            This is the first set that grades whether the output is usable by
            someone who did not write it. Storage Estimate says so in the brief:{" "}
            <i>give me numbers I can read.</i>
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="challenges">
          <h2>The Challenges</h2>

          <p>Pick <b>any five</b>.</p>

          <ul class="list-unstyled mt-4">
            {
              pool.map((c) => (
                <li class="mb-2">
                  <i class="si-terminal text-sn-theme me-2"></i>
                  <a href={`/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/${c.slug}`}>{c.title}</a>
                  <span class="ms-2">
                    {Array.from({ length: c.stars }).map(() => (
                      <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
                    ))}
                  </span>
                  <span class="text-body-secondary ms-2">
                    &mdash; after Activity {c.after}
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>Six in the pool and five required</b>, so there is one you can skip
            and no more. Pick the one you skip on purpose.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="bonus">
          <h2>If Five Is Not Enough</h2>

          <p>
            These two are <b>extra credit</b>, worth 3 points each on top of the
            set.
          </p>

          <ul class="list-unstyled mt-4">
            {
              bonus.map((c) => (
                <li class="mb-2">
                  <i class="si-flask text-sn-yellow me-2"></i>
                  <a href={`/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/${c.slug}`}>{c.title}</a>
                  <span class="ms-2">
                    {Array.from({ length: c.stars }).map(() => (
                      <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
                    ))}
                  </span>
                  <span class="text-body-secondary ms-2">
                    &mdash; after Activity {c.after}
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Correlation Check is the harder of the two, and not because of the
            code.</b> The code is twenty lines. The difficulty is that the story
            you are asked to check might actually be true &mdash; and the data
            still does not prove it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="start-here">
          <h2>If the Part 6 Quiz Went Badly</h2>

          <p>
            These three are the repair kit. <b>They count toward your five</b>
            &mdash; this is not extra work, it is a different order to do it in.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/cc-byte-reader">Byte Reader</a>
              &mdash; bits, numbers, and characters as three readings of one thing
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/cc-overflow-guard">Overflow Guard</a>
              &mdash; why a fixed number of bits has a ceiling
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/cc-compression-picker">Compression Picker</a>
              &mdash; redundancy, ratios, and when compression costs you
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>For <b>each</b> challenge you complete, submit:</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              <b>Your requirements list</b> &mdash; including what your program
              throws away, and what it would get wrong.
            </li>
            <li><b>Your program file.</b></li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            This set is worth <b>5 points</b>, graded on completion. Extra
            credit is on top and does not replace any of the five.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1 class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash">
          Commence Challenges
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
